# Line endings, declared instead of inherited.
#
# Why this file exists: the Git for Windows *system* config
# (C:\Program Files\Git\etc\gitconfig) sets core.autocrlf=true, and this repo has
# no repo-level or user-level override. autocrlf decides per-commit, per-file, based
# on what heuristics find, so the stored form drifted: some blobs hold LF, some hold
# CRLF. The observable cost landed on 2026-09-28 in e94964e - four capacity lines plus
# a GPU-lane edit in execution_plane/docker-compose.yml produced "1217 insertions,
# 1161 deletions", because that file's blob held 1161 CRLF lines and the commit
# normalised all of them. Nothing was wrong with the edit; the history it left is
# unreadable, and every other CRLF-stored file in here will pay the same tax once, on
# whichever commit happens to touch it.
#
# With these rules the answer is the same on every clone and every OS, and a diff
# shows content again.
#
# Scope note: `text=auto` + the rules below do NOT rewrite existing blobs. The
# stored form changes only when a file is next added, which is why the companion
# step is a single deliberate renormalisation commit (see .git-blame-ignore-revs).

* text=auto eol=lf

# This deployment runs in Linux containers, so the checked-out form matches the
# stored form: no surprise conversion on clone.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
Dockerfile* text eol=lf
Makefile text eol=lf
Procfile text eol=lf
*.service text eol=lf
*.conf text eol=lf
*.env text eol=lf
.env* text eol=lf

# cmd.exe parses batch files line by line and LF can misbehave interactively, so
# these two keep CRLF on checkout while still storing LF. PowerShell and bash read
# LF happily, so *.ps1 stays on the default above.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binary is a correctness rule, not a style rule: a "normalised" binary is corrupt
# and the damage is silent. Extend this list for new asset types; do not rely on
# the auto heuristic, which has mistaken model weights and dumps for text before.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.wav binary
*.mp3 binary
*.ogg binary
*.mp4 binary
*.mov binary
*.stl binary
*.3mf binary
*.gcode binary
*.glb binary
*.gltf binary
*.obj binary
*.fbx binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.tgz binary
*.tar binary
*.7z binary
*.exe binary
*.dll binary
*.lib binary
*.pdb binary
*.pyd binary
*.pyc binary
*.db binary
*.sqlite binary
*.sqlite3 binary
*.bin binary
*.onnx binary
*.pt binary
*.pth binary
*.safetensors binary
*.gguf binary
*.ckpt binary
*.lora_weights binary

# Generated / vendored tree that should never be diffed for review.
# graphify-out/ is tracked here and rewrites itself: `graphify update .` regenerates
# graph.json and GRAPH_REPORT.md, and deletes the committed graph.html once the graph
# passes the 5000-node visualiser limit. Marked -diff so a rebuild does not bury the
# real change in review. Deliberately NOT given merge=binary: that would change how
# conflicts resolve, which is a decision to make knowingly or not at all.
graphify-out/** -diff

# Git LFS is installed on this box (filter "lfs" is configured in the system and
# global configs) but NOTHING here declares `filter=lfs`, so no file is in LFS and
# none is being pointer-rewritten. If large assets ever do move to LFS, add the
# `filter=lfs` lines here deliberately - and expect a large migration commit.
